1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,872
Session two.

2
00:00:01,152 --> 00:00:02,273
The eagle-eye view.

3
00:00:02,553 --> 00:00:04,446
Seven system calls make a server.

4
00:00:04,726 --> 00:00:07,371
Seven layers of nesting carry eleven bytes.

5
00:00:07,651 --> 00:00:10,800
The idea that ties the whole
session together is this:

6
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,424
one layer's headers are another layer's body.

7
00:00:14,366 --> 00:00:18,103
A quick recap of session one, with one
new detail about closing.

8
00:00:18,433 --> 00:00:20,024
The server makes seven calls.

9
00:00:20,304 --> 00:00:24,105
The one in listen is the accept queue
depth, not the connection limit.

10
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:26,228
The client skips bind and listen.

11
00:00:26,508 --> 00:00:30,521
The kernel picks an ephemeral source port,
and connect triggers the handshake.

12
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:34,963
A normal close sends a FIN, the polite
way to say I am done sending.

13
00:00:35,243 --> 00:00:40,420
The socket then sits in TIME_WAIT for two times
MSL, the Maximum Segment Lifetime.

14
00:00:40,766 --> 00:00:44,196
SO_LINGER with one and zero sends an RST instead.

15
00:00:44,476 --> 00:00:48,205
Immediate teardown, unsent data
discarded, no TIME_WAIT.

16
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:52,172
The OSI model is a nineteen eighty-four committee

17
00:00:52,172 --> 00:00:55,272
model that outlived almost everything built on it.

18
00:00:55,599 --> 00:01:00,777
Layer seven, application: HTTP, FTP, DNS, SMTP.

19
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:04,878
Layer six, presentation:
encryption, compression, encoding.

20
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:08,875
Layer five, session: session
management and authentication.

21
00:01:09,199 --> 00:01:12,385
Layer four, transport: TCP and UDP.

22
00:01:12,733 --> 00:01:16,264
Layer three, network: IP, ICMP, and routing.

23
00:01:16,599 --> 00:01:20,553
Layer two, data link: Ethernet, MAC
addresses, ARP, switches.

24
00:01:20,899 --> 00:01:23,929
Layer one, physical: cables, signals, hardware.

25
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:28,262
Both live at layer four, but they
make opposite promises.

26
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:31,471
TCP is connection-oriented.

27
00:01:31,751 --> 00:01:33,407
UDP is connectionless.

28
00:01:33,733 --> 00:01:36,515
TCP gives reliable, ordered delivery.

29
00:01:36,795 --> 00:01:39,762
UDP gives no guarantee and no ordering.

30
00:01:40,099 --> 00:01:44,540
TCP adds flow control, congestion
control and error recovery.

31
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:47,160
UDP is fast, with low overhead.

32
00:01:47,499 --> 00:01:51,966
So TCP has higher latency, and
UDP has low latency.

33
00:01:52,299 --> 00:01:57,002
HTTP, FTP, SMTP and SSH use TCP.

34
00:01:57,282 --> 00:02:01,275
DNS, voice calls, video calls and games use UDP.

35
00:02:02,199 --> 00:02:05,690
Layers one and two differ a lot
between a wire and radio.

36
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:08,609
Ethernet, over a wire, detects collisions.

37
00:02:08,889 --> 00:02:10,349
That is CSMA/CD.

38
00:02:10,629 --> 00:02:13,404
Wi-Fi, over radio, can only avoid them.

39
00:02:13,684 --> 00:02:15,123
That is CSMA/CA.

40
00:02:15,466 --> 00:02:17,048
Why not detect on radio?

41
00:02:17,328 --> 00:02:19,944
Because a radio cannot listen while it transmits.

42
00:02:20,224 --> 00:02:22,701
Its own signal drowns everything else out.

43
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:25,095
So CSMA/CA works in steps.

44
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:26,950
First, listen to the channel.

45
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:28,489
This is carrier sense.

46
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:30,159
If it is busy, defer.

47
00:02:30,499 --> 00:02:33,555
Then wait a random backoff, called
the contention window.

48
00:02:33,899 --> 00:02:34,884
Then transmit.

49
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:38,299
And wait for an explicit
acknowledgement for every frame.

50
00:02:38,579 --> 00:02:40,824
No ACK means back off and retransmit.

51
00:02:41,166 --> 00:02:45,995
All of this costs about two milliseconds of
contention delay before a frame leaves.

52
00:02:46,275 --> 00:02:49,593
And every Wi-Fi frame is
acknowledged at layer two,

53
00:02:49,593 --> 00:02:53,105
long before TCP at layer four
knows anything happened.

54
00:02:54,033 --> 00:02:58,217
Your packet can go to space and back, and
nothing above layer two notices.

55
00:02:58,566 --> 00:02:59,416
A laptop.

56
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:01,390
Over Wi-Fi or Ethernet.

57
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:03,539
To a Starlink phased-array dish.

58
00:03:03,866 --> 00:03:08,249
An RF uplink in the Ku or Ka band to a
low earth orbit satellite,

59
00:03:08,249 --> 00:03:12,767
about five hundred fifty kilometres up,
around twenty milliseconds,

60
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:16,611
optionally hopping across laser
links between satellites.

61
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:18,294
Down to a ground gateway.

62
00:03:18,633 --> 00:03:19,783
And onto the internet.

63
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:21,430
Nine physical hops.

64
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:26,500
The IP header arrives unchanged, except for
the time to live, the checksum,

65
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:27,777
and any NAT rewrite.

66
00:03:28,057 --> 00:03:30,104
That is the whole argument for layering.

67
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:33,085
Now what a packet actually looks like.

68
00:03:33,365 --> 00:03:34,948
Each layer wraps the one above.

69
00:03:35,266 --> 00:03:36,265
Start at the top.

70
00:03:36,545 --> 00:03:42,758
Layer seven is the HTTP request, an opaque byte
stream that TCP does not care about.

71
00:03:43,099 --> 00:03:45,624
TCP wraps it in a twenty byte header.

72
00:03:45,904 --> 00:03:50,583
Source port two bytes, destination port two
bytes, sequence number four bytes,

73
00:03:50,583 --> 00:03:54,003
acknowledgement number four bytes,
then flags and window.

74
00:03:54,333 --> 00:03:56,836
IP wraps that in another twenty byte header.

75
00:03:57,116 --> 00:04:02,758
It carries a time to live of one byte, a protocol
field where six means TCP, a checksum,

76
00:04:02,758 --> 00:04:06,541
and four bytes each for the source
and destination address.

77
00:04:06,866 --> 00:04:09,437
Ethernet adds a fourteen byte header in front.

78
00:04:09,717 --> 00:04:15,025
Destination MAC six bytes, source MAC six
bytes, and a two byte EtherType,

79
00:04:15,025 --> 00:04:18,254
where zero x zero eight zero zero means IPv4.

80
00:04:18,599 --> 00:04:22,761
And a four byte frame check sequence at
the end, a CRC thirty-two.

81
00:04:23,099 --> 00:04:24,141
The byte budget.

82
00:04:24,421 --> 00:04:26,216
The MTU is fifteen hundred.

83
00:04:26,496 --> 00:04:29,477
Subtract twenty for IP and twenty for TCP,

84
00:04:29,477 --> 00:04:33,239
and you get a maximum segment
size of fourteen sixty.

85
00:04:33,519 --> 00:04:36,830
Add the fourteen byte Ethernet
header and four byte trailer,

86
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:39,701
and the frame on the wire is
fifteen eighteen bytes.

87
00:04:40,633 --> 00:04:42,144
Now a very different network.

88
00:04:42,424 --> 00:04:44,850
SS7, from nineteen seventy-five.

89
00:04:45,130 --> 00:04:48,442
It is a second internet, older than TCP IP.

90
00:04:48,766 --> 00:04:51,966
Its one big idea: get signalling
out of the voice channel.

91
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:53,904
Before: in-band signalling.

92
00:04:54,184 --> 00:04:56,296
Voice and control shared one channel.

93
00:04:56,576 --> 00:05:00,075
A twenty-six hundred hertz tone
meant this trunk is idle,

94
00:05:00,075 --> 00:05:03,082
and digits were dialled as multi-frequency tones.

95
00:05:03,362 --> 00:05:06,272
Anyone who could make the sound
could make the decision.

96
00:05:06,552 --> 00:05:08,796
These were the blue boxes of phone phreaking.

97
00:05:09,133 --> 00:05:10,388
After: out-of-band.

98
00:05:10,668 --> 00:05:15,809
The voice bearer is a sixty-four kilobit DS
zero carrying only the conversation.

99
00:05:16,089 --> 00:05:19,331
A separate packet-switched signalling
network carries the control.

100
00:05:19,611 --> 00:05:23,279
That is the control plane versus
data plane separation,

101
00:05:23,279 --> 00:05:25,880
decades before anyone used those words.

102
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:32,912
Compare the SS7 stack with TCP IP, stack to
stack, not protocol to protocol.

103
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:40,993
At layer seven, SS7 has MAP, INAP, CAP and
ISUP: call setup, roaming and SMS.

104
00:05:41,273 --> 00:05:45,876
TCP IP has HTTP, DNS, SMTP and SIP.

105
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:52,929
At layers five and six, SS7 has TCAP, which
handles dialogues using ASN.1 BER.

106
00:05:53,209 --> 00:05:56,146
TCP IP has no distinct layer here.

107
00:05:56,466 --> 00:06:00,537
At layer four, SCCP, which does
Global Title Translation,

108
00:06:00,537 --> 00:06:03,393
mapping a phone number to a destination.

109
00:06:03,673 --> 00:06:06,692
TCP IP has TCP and UDP.

110
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:10,091
At layer three, MTP three routes by point code.

111
00:06:10,371 --> 00:06:12,617
IP is best effort with a time to live.

112
00:06:12,966 --> 00:06:16,199
At layer two, MTP two handles signal units,

113
00:06:16,199 --> 00:06:20,335
sequence numbers and a CRC
sixteen with retransmission.

114
00:06:20,615 --> 00:06:22,956
Ethernet does framing and an FCS.

115
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:29,786
And at layer one, MTP one is a sixty-four
kilobit DS zero on an E one or T one line.

116
00:06:30,733 --> 00:06:36,406
Let us encapsulate a real text message:
Hello World, sent as a MO ForwardSM.

117
00:06:36,733 --> 00:06:40,456
The outermost layer is the MTP
two message signal unit.

118
00:06:40,736 --> 00:06:44,190
Its payload field is capped at two
hundred seventy-three octets.

119
00:06:44,533 --> 00:06:48,721
Inside it, MTP three: service
indicator zero x eight three,

120
00:06:48,721 --> 00:06:54,756
destination point code two thousand sixty-five,
origin point code two thousand fifty,

121
00:06:54,756 --> 00:06:56,104
link selector five.

122
00:06:56,433 --> 00:06:59,772
Inside that, an SCCP unitdata message, class one,

123
00:06:59,772 --> 00:07:03,929
whose called and calling Global
Titles are the phone numbers.

124
00:07:04,266 --> 00:07:09,403
Inside that, a TCAP BEGIN carrying an
Invoke with operation code forty-six,

125
00:07:09,403 --> 00:07:10,910
which is mo_ForwardSM.

126
00:07:11,233 --> 00:07:14,195
Then the MAP MO ForwardSM argument.

127
00:07:14,533 --> 00:07:20,700
Then the SMS-SUBMIT TPDU, with data coding
scheme zero meaning GSM seven bit,

128
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:23,343
and a user data length of twelve.

129
00:07:23,666 --> 00:07:30,483
And finally the user data: eleven octets, C eight,
thirty-two, nine B, FD, zero six, five D,

130
00:07:30,483 --> 00:07:34,335
DF, seventy-two, thirty-six,
thirty-nine, zero four.

131
00:07:34,615 --> 00:07:35,932
That spells Hello World.

132
00:07:36,266 --> 00:07:41,543
Same picture as Ethernet around IP
around TCP around HTTP.

133
00:07:41,823 --> 00:07:43,814
A different network, the same idea.

134
00:07:44,733 --> 00:07:47,295
Why is an SMS one hundred forty bytes?

135
00:07:47,575 --> 00:07:49,952
Three stacked limits, and the smallest wins.

136
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:54,444
Two hundred seventy-two octets: the
signal information field,

137
00:07:54,444 --> 00:07:57,978
the hard ceiling of one MTP two
message signal unit.

138
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:03,972
About two hundred octets: the MAP sm RP UI
field that carries the message.

139
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:09,128
And one hundred forty octets: the TP
user data, the SMS itself.

140
00:08:09,408 --> 00:08:14,940
It was chosen so the whole MAP operation
always fits in one message signal unit.

141
00:08:15,266 --> 00:08:18,126
So there is no segmentation and no reassembly.

142
00:08:18,406 --> 00:08:20,415
One text is one signalling packet.

143
00:08:20,695 --> 00:08:26,419
That is why SMS ran on nineteen eighties hardware,
and still works when mobile data is down.

144
00:08:27,366 --> 00:08:31,165
How do you fit one hundred sixty
characters into one hundred forty bytes?

145
00:08:31,445 --> 00:08:33,114
Pack seven bits per character.

146
00:08:33,433 --> 00:08:37,522
One hundred forty octets times eight is
eleven hundred twenty bits.

147
00:08:37,802 --> 00:08:42,773
Divide by seven bits per character, and you get
exactly one hundred sixty characters.

148
00:08:43,053 --> 00:08:47,737
This is GSM seven bit packing: septets
in a continuous bit stream.

149
00:08:48,066 --> 00:08:53,610
Twelve characters times seven bits is eighty-four
bits, which rounds up to eleven octets,

150
00:08:53,610 --> 00:08:55,541
with four bits of zero padding.

151
00:08:55,866 --> 00:09:01,673
Switch to UCS two for emoji or non-Latin
scripts, and it is two bytes per character:

152
00:09:01,673 --> 00:09:05,475
one hundred forty divided by two
is seventy characters.

153
00:09:05,755 --> 00:09:10,770
One emoji can more than halve your message,
and silently cost you a second SMS.

154
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:15,679
Sending an SMS is really a database
lookup wrapped in a queue.

155
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,060
Phone A submits an SMS-SUBMIT.

156
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:24,189
It reaches the visited MSC and VLR,
which sends a MAP mo-ForwardSM.

157
00:09:24,533 --> 00:09:26,799
To the SMS centre, which stores it.

158
00:09:27,079 --> 00:09:30,231
It replies TCAP END immediately: accepted.

159
00:09:30,511 --> 00:09:33,149
Delivery is now the SMSC's problem.

160
00:09:33,466 --> 00:09:39,282
The SMSC asks the HLR, the subscriber
database: send routing info for SM,

161
00:09:39,282 --> 00:09:41,035
with the phone number.

162
00:09:41,366 --> 00:09:45,748
The HLR returns the IMSI and the
address of the serving MSC.

163
00:09:46,028 --> 00:09:52,854
The SMSC then sends a MAP mt-ForwardSM,
an SMS-DELIVER, to that MSC.

164
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:54,767
Which pushes it to Phone B.

165
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:57,120
Delivery acknowledgement flows back.

166
00:09:57,400 --> 00:10:02,166
An SMS status report is generated only
if the sender requested it.

167
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:07,515
A phone call works the same way: signalling
on one path, voice on another.

168
00:10:08,533 --> 00:10:10,305
Phone A goes off-hook and dials.

169
00:10:10,585 --> 00:10:14,059
MSC A sends an IAM, the Initial Address Message.

170
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:18,367
MSC B rings phone B and replies
ACM, Address Complete.

171
00:10:18,647 --> 00:10:19,925
The caller hears ringback.

172
00:10:20,266 --> 00:10:22,808
B answers: ANM, the Answer Message.

173
00:10:23,088 --> 00:10:24,517
The voice path is cut through.

174
00:10:24,866 --> 00:10:28,977
Now the conversation travels on the
sixty-four kilobit bearer circuit.

175
00:10:29,257 --> 00:10:32,112
SS7 is not involved at all while you talk.

176
00:10:32,433 --> 00:10:35,197
When someone hangs up, a REL, Release, is sent.

177
00:10:35,533 --> 00:10:37,882
Answered by RLC, Release Complete.

178
00:10:38,162 --> 00:10:42,044
Four messages set up a call, and
signalling is idle during the talk.

179
00:10:42,966 --> 00:10:49,969
On 5G and IMS, the same call is made with
SIP: text shaped like HTTP, over IP.

180
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,427
Alice sends INVITE with an SDP
offer, through the proxy.

181
00:10:54,766 --> 00:10:56,271
Back comes one hundred Trying,

182
00:10:56,600 --> 00:10:57,544
one eighty Ringing,

183
00:10:57,866 --> 00:11:00,524
and two hundred OK with an SDP answer.

184
00:11:00,804 --> 00:11:03,318
SIP borrowed HTTP's status codes.

185
00:11:03,666 --> 00:11:05,833
Alice confirms with ACK, end to end.

186
00:11:06,166 --> 00:11:12,765
Now the media flows as RTP or SRTP over UDP,
directly between the endpoints.

187
00:11:13,045 --> 00:11:14,545
The proxy never touches it.

188
00:11:14,825 --> 00:11:17,124
Control and data, on separate paths.

189
00:11:17,466 --> 00:11:19,395
Then BYE, and a two hundred OK.

190
00:11:20,333 --> 00:11:22,745
SS7 makes every hop reliable.

191
00:11:23,025 --> 00:11:26,002
TCP makes only the endpoints reliable.

192
00:11:27,033 --> 00:11:27,861
Sequencing.

193
00:11:28,141 --> 00:11:33,420
SS7 uses a seven bit forward sequence number
that wraps at one twenty-eight.

194
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:37,794
TCP uses a thirty-two bit sequence
number, counted per byte.

195
00:11:38,133 --> 00:11:38,690
Acks.

196
00:11:38,970 --> 00:11:41,808
SS7 piggybacks a backward sequence number.

197
00:11:42,088 --> 00:11:45,604
TCP uses a cumulative ack number, plus SACK.

198
00:11:45,933 --> 00:11:46,968
Retransmission.

199
00:11:47,248 --> 00:11:48,880
SS7 toggles a bit.

200
00:11:49,160 --> 00:11:55,197
TCP uses retransmission timers, and fast
retransmit on three duplicate acks.

201
00:11:55,533 --> 00:11:56,476
Flow control.

202
00:11:56,756 --> 00:11:58,838
SS7 is blunt stop or go.

203
00:11:59,118 --> 00:12:03,274
TCP has the sliding window
advertised in every segment.

204
00:12:03,599 --> 00:12:04,511
Error detection.

205
00:12:04,791 --> 00:12:08,887
SS7 uses a CRC sixteen on every signal unit.

206
00:12:09,167 --> 00:12:11,830
TCP uses a sixteen bit checksum.

207
00:12:12,166 --> 00:12:12,900
Addressing.

208
00:12:13,180 --> 00:12:17,503
SS7 uses point codes, subsystem
numbers and Global Titles.

209
00:12:17,783 --> 00:12:20,085
TCP uses IP and port.

210
00:12:20,433 --> 00:12:21,688
Congestion control.

211
00:12:21,968 --> 00:12:23,726
SS7 is hop by hop.

212
00:12:24,006 --> 00:12:25,638
TCP is end to end.

213
00:12:25,966 --> 00:12:30,772
So the scope of reliability: SS7
per hop, TCP end to end.

214
00:12:31,099 --> 00:12:36,937
And the maximum payload: two hundred seventy-two
octets, versus about fourteen sixty bytes,

215
00:12:36,937 --> 00:12:39,759
or sixty-four kilobytes with window scaling.

216
00:12:40,699 --> 00:12:45,085
Next, congestion control, and
Google's BBR from twenty sixteen.

217
00:12:45,433 --> 00:12:50,888
Loss-based TCP, like Reno and CUBIC, grows
until a packet drops, then halves.

218
00:12:51,168 --> 00:12:52,376
That makes a sawtooth.

219
00:12:52,656 --> 00:12:57,814
It needs loss to make progress, so on lossy,
wireless or long fat links they crawl.

220
00:12:58,094 --> 00:13:00,656
And they fill buffers, which causes bufferbloat.

221
00:13:00,999 --> 00:13:05,576
BBR measures the bottleneck bandwidth and
the round-trip propagation time,

222
00:13:05,576 --> 00:13:09,719
and sends at what the path can carry,
keeping the queue near empty.

223
00:13:09,999 --> 00:13:14,388
Google reported up to two thousand times
the throughput on some benchmarks,

224
00:13:14,388 --> 00:13:15,734
and much lower latency.

225
00:13:16,666 --> 00:13:18,138
The TCP handshake.

226
00:13:18,418 --> 00:13:21,638
Each direction needs its own
sequence number confirmed.

227
00:13:22,666 --> 00:13:23,951
The app calls connect.

228
00:13:24,231 --> 00:13:29,878
The client sends a SYN with sequence number one
thousand, and options: maximum segment size,

229
00:13:29,878 --> 00:13:31,657
SACK permitted, window scale.

230
00:13:31,999 --> 00:13:36,282
The server replies SYN ACK with its own
sequence number, five thousand,

231
00:13:36,282 --> 00:13:37,790
and ack one thousand one.

232
00:13:38,070 --> 00:13:40,660
It now holds a half-open entry in the backlog.

233
00:13:40,999 --> 00:13:42,256
The client sends ACK.

234
00:13:42,536 --> 00:13:43,887
No payload is needed.

235
00:13:44,167 --> 00:13:47,624
Both sides are established, and the
server's accept returns.

236
00:13:49,166 --> 00:13:52,123
The server's own SYN is what forces the third leg.

237
00:13:52,466 --> 00:13:55,440
A SYN and a FIN each consume one sequence number.

238
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:59,667
That is why ack one thousand one
answers sequence one thousand,

239
00:13:59,667 --> 00:14:01,922
even though the SYN carried no data.

240
00:14:02,266 --> 00:14:04,782
And the initial sequence number must be random.

241
00:14:05,062 --> 00:14:09,603
Early stacks were predictable, so an attacker
could inject forged segments blind.

242
00:14:09,883 --> 00:14:11,236
That was the Mitnick attack.

243
00:14:11,516 --> 00:14:17,269
RFC sixty-five twenty-eight fixes it with a keyed
hash of the four tuple plus a timer.

244
00:14:18,199 --> 00:14:20,581
An attack on that handshake: the SYN flood.

245
00:14:21,599 --> 00:14:25,421
The attacker sends spoofed SYNs and
never completes the handshake.

246
00:14:25,766 --> 00:14:28,234
Each one costs the attacker one packet,

247
00:14:28,234 --> 00:14:32,411
and costs the server a half-open
backlog entry until it times out.

248
00:14:32,691 --> 00:14:35,895
Fill the backlog, and legitimate
connections are refused.

249
00:14:36,175 --> 00:14:38,431
Remember listen with a backlog of one.

250
00:14:39,366 --> 00:14:41,628
The defence is to keep no state at all.

251
00:14:41,966 --> 00:14:47,318
Encode the connection parameters from the four
tuple, a coarse timestamp and the MSS,

252
00:14:47,666 --> 00:14:49,109
run them through a keyed hash,

253
00:14:49,433 --> 00:14:52,759
and use the result as the server's
initial sequence number.

254
00:14:53,099 --> 00:14:55,094
The client's ACK returns it plus one.

255
00:14:55,433 --> 00:14:59,183
The server recomputes, validates, and
reconstructs the connection.

256
00:14:59,463 --> 00:15:01,305
No memory was spent in between.

257
00:15:01,633 --> 00:15:07,290
The cost: only thirty-two bits of ISN, so
most TCP options, like window scale,

258
00:15:07,290 --> 00:15:09,974
SACK and timestamps, are mostly lost.

259
00:15:10,254 --> 00:15:12,856
That is why cookies switch on only under pressure.

260
00:15:13,199 --> 00:15:18,175
The same trick shows up everywhere: push the state
into a token that the client hands back.

261
00:15:18,455 --> 00:15:21,981
HTTP cookies, JWTs, signed URLs.

262
00:15:22,899 --> 00:15:25,755
Closing a connection takes four
segments, not three.

263
00:15:26,799 --> 00:15:28,056
The client sends a FIN.

264
00:15:28,399 --> 00:15:29,805
The server acknowledges.

265
00:15:30,085 --> 00:15:33,798
The server is now in CLOSE_WAIT, and
the client in FIN_WAIT two.

266
00:15:34,078 --> 00:15:36,853
This is half closed: the client can still receive.

267
00:15:37,199 --> 00:15:40,442
The server sends any remaining
data, and then its own FIN.

268
00:15:40,766 --> 00:15:46,235
The client acknowledges, and waits in TIME_WAIT
for two MSL before it is finally closed.

269
00:15:47,766 --> 00:15:50,442
SYN and ACK can be combined into one segment.

270
00:15:50,722 --> 00:15:54,629
FIN and ACK cannot, because the other
side may still have data to send.

271
00:15:54,966 --> 00:16:00,057
Sockets stuck in CLOSE_WAIT are almost always an
application that forgot to call close.

272
00:16:00,337 --> 00:16:01,958
It is not a network problem.

273
00:16:02,238 --> 00:16:03,157
Look at your code.

274
00:16:03,499 --> 00:16:07,675
And TIME_WAIT is why restarting a server
says address already in use,

275
00:16:07,675 --> 00:16:09,188
which SO_REUSEADDR fixes.

276
00:16:10,133 --> 00:16:11,371
The round trip tax.

277
00:16:11,651 --> 00:16:14,374
How many round trips before the
first application byte?

278
00:16:15,399 --> 00:16:17,110
Plain TCP: one.

279
00:16:17,433 --> 00:16:20,171
TCP plus TLS one point two: three.

280
00:16:20,451 --> 00:16:25,507
The TCP handshake, then ClientHello and
ServerHello, then key exchange.

281
00:16:25,833 --> 00:16:30,472
TLS one point three saves one round
trip of crypto: two in total.

282
00:16:30,799 --> 00:16:35,125
TCP Fast Open puts data in the SYN
for returning clients.

283
00:16:35,466 --> 00:16:38,449
And QUIC folds transport and crypto together: one.

284
00:16:38,766 --> 00:16:40,010
Bandwidth you can buy.

285
00:16:40,290 --> 00:16:41,604
Round trips you cannot.

286
00:16:41,884 --> 00:16:46,525
Mumbai to Virginia is about one hundred
ninety milliseconds, every round trip.

287
00:16:47,466 --> 00:16:50,684
UDP is TCP with every promise removed.

288
00:16:51,033 --> 00:16:53,609
The UDP header is eight bytes in total.

289
00:16:53,889 --> 00:16:55,268
Source port, two bytes.

290
00:16:55,548 --> 00:16:57,426
Destination port, two bytes.

291
00:16:57,706 --> 00:16:58,841
Length, two bytes.

292
00:16:59,121 --> 00:17:00,379
Checksum, two bytes.

293
00:17:00,659 --> 00:17:04,486
The payload goes up to sixty-five
thousand five hundred seven bytes.

294
00:17:04,833 --> 00:17:09,422
Compare TCP: at least twenty bytes, with
sequence, ack, flags, window,

295
00:17:09,422 --> 00:17:11,585
and up to forty bytes of options.

296
00:17:11,933 --> 00:17:17,578
Zoom, WebRTC, game servers, DNS and
real-time streaming use UDP.

297
00:17:17,858 --> 00:17:22,589
A retransmitted audio frame arrives too late
to play, so dropping it is correct.

298
00:17:23,533 --> 00:17:27,223
QUIC rebuilds TCP inside UDP, in userspace.

299
00:17:27,566 --> 00:17:32,831
With HTTP one and two over TCP, TLS
is a separate handshake,

300
00:17:32,831 --> 00:17:35,902
and TCP is one ordered byte stream.

301
00:17:36,182 --> 00:17:38,233
One lost packet stalls every stream.

302
00:17:38,513 --> 00:17:39,977
That is head-of-line blocking.

303
00:17:40,299 --> 00:17:44,757
HTTP three over QUIC gives each
stream its own sequence space,

304
00:17:44,757 --> 00:17:48,640
and folds TLS one point three
into the same handshake.

305
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:51,273
Lose a packet, and only that stream waits.

306
00:17:51,553 --> 00:17:55,661
UDP's eight bytes are left alone by
kernels and middleboxes.

307
00:17:55,999 --> 00:17:59,174
Why userspace matters: TCP lives in the kernel,

308
00:17:59,174 --> 00:18:03,835
so changing it means an operating system
update for the whole planet,

309
00:18:03,835 --> 00:18:06,334
and hoping middleboxes do not object.

310
00:18:06,666 --> 00:18:11,723
QUIC ships in the browser, so Google can deploy a
congestion control change on a Tuesday.

311
00:18:12,666 --> 00:18:15,035
Now text protocols you can speak by hand.

312
00:18:15,315 --> 00:18:16,712
First SMTP.

313
00:18:16,992 --> 00:18:18,311
The S stands for Simple.

314
00:18:18,591 --> 00:18:20,183
It has never stood for Secure.

315
00:18:21,233 --> 00:18:24,987
Port twenty-five is relay, server
to server, in plaintext.

316
00:18:25,333 --> 00:18:30,794
Port five eighty-seven is submission, client
to server, upgraded with STARTTLS.

317
00:18:31,074 --> 00:18:31,928
Use this one.

318
00:18:32,266 --> 00:18:37,102
Port four sixty-five is SMTPS: TLS
from the very first byte.

319
00:18:37,433 --> 00:18:42,392
And one-ten and one-forty-three are for
retrieving mail: POP three and IMAP.

320
00:18:43,333 --> 00:18:45,417
Here is a session by hand over telnet.

321
00:18:45,766 --> 00:18:50,832
EHLO, then MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, the
headers, a blank line, the body,

322
00:18:50,832 --> 00:18:53,815
a single dot on a line by itself, and QUIT.

323
00:18:54,133 --> 00:18:55,470
Envelope versus letter.

324
00:18:55,750 --> 00:18:59,786
MAIL FROM and RCPT TO are the
envelope, used for routing.

325
00:19:00,133 --> 00:19:03,478
From and To inside DATA are just
text printed on the letter.

326
00:19:03,758 --> 00:19:05,884
They do not have to match, and nothing checks.

327
00:19:06,164 --> 00:19:09,126
There is no authentication in base SMTP.

328
00:19:09,466 --> 00:19:14,815
A lone dot ends the message, so a body line that
starts with a dot gets an extra dot.

329
00:19:15,095 --> 00:19:16,369
That is dot-stuffing.

330
00:19:16,699 --> 00:19:19,796
Miss the blank line, and the
subject becomes the body.

331
00:19:20,133 --> 00:19:26,113
STARTTLS, SPF, DKIM and DMARC are all later
patches, answering the question:

332
00:19:26,113 --> 00:19:28,867
is this sender allowed to say that?

333
00:19:29,799 --> 00:19:32,339
How do images travel over a text protocol?

334
00:19:32,619 --> 00:19:34,137
MIME and base sixty-four.

335
00:19:34,466 --> 00:19:38,361
Three bytes, twenty-four bits, are
regrouped as four six-bit values.

336
00:19:38,641 --> 00:19:41,779
Each maps to one of sixty-four
safe printable characters.

337
00:19:42,059 --> 00:19:44,317
The word Man becomes TWF u.

338
00:19:44,666 --> 00:19:48,532
The alphabet is upper and lower case
letters, digits, plus and slash,

339
00:19:48,532 --> 00:19:49,876
with equals for padding.

340
00:19:50,156 --> 00:19:53,637
The size grows by four thirds,
about thirty-three percent,

341
00:19:53,637 --> 00:19:56,218
wrapped at seventy-six characters per line.

342
00:19:56,498 --> 00:20:00,292
Parts are separated by a boundary
declared in the Content-Type header.

343
00:20:00,572 --> 00:20:04,616
Each part starts with --and the boundary, and
the last ends with a trailing -dash.

344
00:20:04,896 --> 00:20:08,669
Each part declares Content-Transfer-
Encoding base sixty-four.

345
00:20:09,599 --> 00:20:10,443
Reading mail.

346
00:20:10,723 --> 00:20:16,249
POP three has four verbs, on port one-ten,
or nine ninety-five with TLS.

347
00:20:17,266 --> 00:20:23,918
Log in with USER and PASS, in plaintext, which is
why POP3S on port nine ninety-five exists.

348
00:20:24,266 --> 00:20:26,578
STAT returns the count and total bytes.

349
00:20:26,899 --> 00:20:29,887
LIST returns a number and size for each message.

350
00:20:30,233 --> 00:20:32,219
RETR n fetches the whole message.

351
00:20:32,566 --> 00:20:36,493
DELE n marks it for deletion, and the
deletion is applied at QUIT.

352
00:20:37,433 --> 00:20:39,502
POP puts the state on your machine.

353
00:20:39,782 --> 00:20:41,318
IMAP puts it on the server.

354
00:20:42,366 --> 00:20:46,980
With POP the mail lives on your device and
the server is a spool to empty.

355
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:50,818
With IMAP it lives on the server and
the client holds a cache.

356
00:20:51,166 --> 00:20:53,780
A second device sees nothing under POP.

357
00:20:54,060 --> 00:20:57,334
Under IMAP it sees the same
mailbox with the same state.

358
00:20:57,666 --> 00:21:00,693
Read and unread is local only under POP.

359
00:21:00,973 --> 00:21:04,629
IMAP keeps server-side flags:
Seen, Answered, Flagged.

360
00:21:04,966 --> 00:21:06,862
POP has just the inbox.

361
00:21:07,142 --> 00:21:10,418
IMAP has an arbitrary folder
hierarchy over the wire.

362
00:21:10,766 --> 00:21:13,883
POP downloads everything and searches locally.

363
00:21:14,163 --> 00:21:17,340
IMAP searches on the server, and
IDLE pushes new mail.

364
00:21:17,666 --> 00:21:20,351
And POP can only fetch the whole message.

365
00:21:20,631 --> 00:21:22,822
IMAP can fetch just one MIME part.

366
00:21:23,766 --> 00:21:25,493
A recurring theme: framing.

367
00:21:25,773 --> 00:21:28,904
Every protocol picks a
delimiter, a length, or both.

368
00:21:29,933 --> 00:21:36,629
Delimiters: SMTP's lone dot, HTTP's blank
line, MIME boundaries, Redis CRLF.

369
00:21:36,966 --> 00:21:43,558
Lengths: an SMS user data length, HTTP content
length, the MTP two length indicator,

370
00:21:43,558 --> 00:21:45,520
protobuf's varint prefix.

371
00:21:45,866 --> 00:21:48,500
Both: IMAP literals say how many bytes follow.

372
00:21:48,780 --> 00:21:52,610
POP three has a plus OK line and
then a dotted body.

373
00:21:52,890 --> 00:21:55,606
HTTP two uses binary frames.

374
00:21:57,533 --> 00:22:01,917
A delimiter is cheap and readable, and you
can stream without knowing the size,

375
00:22:01,917 --> 00:22:03,803
but you must escape the delimiter.

376
00:22:04,083 --> 00:22:05,050
Dot-stuffing.

377
00:22:05,399 --> 00:22:08,926
A length means content can never be
confused with protocol,

378
00:22:08,926 --> 00:22:11,436
but you must know the size before sending.

379
00:22:11,716 --> 00:22:14,172
That is why chunked transfer encoding exists.

380
00:22:14,499 --> 00:22:20,007
Using both gives readable control and a safe binary
payload, but you now have two parsers,

381
00:22:20,007 --> 00:22:23,189
and request smuggling lives in
the gap between them.

382
00:22:24,133 --> 00:22:28,011
Finally, FTP, which refuses to
mix commands and data.

383
00:22:28,333 --> 00:22:35,593
FTP uses a control connection on port twenty-one,
for commands like USER, PASS, LIST, RETR,

384
00:22:35,593 --> 00:22:36,630
PWD and PASV.

385
00:22:36,910 --> 00:22:40,208
And a brand new data connection for
every file or listing.

386
00:22:40,533 --> 00:22:42,764
Active mode, from nineteen seventy-one:

387
00:22:42,764 --> 00:22:46,083
the server connects from port
twenty back into the client.

388
00:22:46,363 --> 00:22:48,588
Every NAT and firewall now blocks this.

389
00:22:48,933 --> 00:22:52,904
Passive mode, PASV: the server replies
with an address and port,

390
00:22:52,904 --> 00:22:55,262
and the client opens both connections.

391
00:22:55,542 --> 00:22:59,738
That is the only shape NAT allows, so it
is effectively mandatory now.

392
00:23:01,266 --> 00:23:06,130
To decode the two twenty-seven reply: the
first four numbers are the IP address.

393
00:23:06,410 --> 00:23:09,536
The port is p one times two fifty-six plus p two.

394
00:23:09,816 --> 00:23:14,132
One seventeen times two fifty-six plus
forty-eight is thirty thousand.

395
00:23:14,412 --> 00:23:17,477
A big-endian pair, htons spelled out in ASCII.

396
00:23:18,999 --> 00:23:20,286
To keep from this session.

397
00:23:20,566 --> 00:23:23,598
Encapsulation: the same picture in both stacks.

398
00:23:23,933 --> 00:23:29,046
Framing: SMS chose a length, SMTP
chose a dot, IMAP chose both.

399
00:23:29,366 --> 00:23:35,722
Reliability has an address: SS7 at layer two
per hop, TCP at layer four end to end,

400
00:23:35,722 --> 00:23:37,100
QUIC in userspace.

401
00:23:37,433 --> 00:23:44,011
And separate control from data: SS7 killed blue
boxes, SIP keeps media off the proxy,

402
00:23:44,011 --> 00:23:46,024
FTP opens a second socket.

403
00:23:46,966 --> 00:23:50,306
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session two.

404
00:24:13,833 --> 00:24:14,967
That is session two.

405
00:24:15,247 --> 00:24:19,860
Next up, session three: scaling servers,
from one process to many clients.

